## Structured chats + tools

Note, tool-calling not supported in structured chats.

Workaround is two steps 1) use tool in unstructed chat

2)  follow up with structured chat

# Principles

## Phenotyping tasks

> A problem well stated is a problem half solved.

\br

. . .

> Nowadays, a problem well stated is a problem 95% solved.

## Phenotyping tasks

Today we're going to use LLMs for lower-stakes, human-in-the-loop, phenotyping tasks:

-  Drafting clinical descriptions

\br

. . .

-  Reviewing concepts to include

\br

. . .

-  Contextualising PhenotypeR results

\br

. . .

Higher-risk topics we won't cover (maybe for next year): 

-  Searching for codes. 

-  Creating cohort logic. 

-  End-to-end, black box, phenotyping


# Clinical descriptions


## Specification


```{r, message=TRUE}
clinicalDescriptionSpecification() |> 
  listviewer::jsonedit()
```



## Specification



```{r, eval=FALSE}
shinyClinicalDescriptions(directory = tempdir())
```



## Drafting clinical descriptions using an LLM  



```{r, message=TRUE}
hypertension_cd <- draftClinicalDescription(chat_medgemma, "hypertension", tempdir())
hypertension_cd
```



## Drafting clinical descriptions using an LLM  



```{r, message=TRUE}
hypertension_cd <- draftClinicalDescription(chat_medgemma, 
                                            c("Primary hypertension",
                                              "Secondary hypertension",
                                              "Resistant hypertension"), 
                                            tempdir())
```

```{r, message=TRUE}
hypertension_cd$`Primary hypertension`$introduction_synonyms
```

```{r, message=TRUE}
hypertension_cd$`Secondary hypertension`$introduction_synonyms
```

```{r, message=TRUE}
hypertension_cd$`Resistant hypertension`$introduction_synonyms
```



## Extra context: data source descriptions

Data partner descriptions can be used to provide extra context



```{r, eval=FALSE}
shinyDataSourceDescriptions(directory = tempdir())
```



